-- #1111 A-2.13 (#1319 unit 3) — #1376's drain, on all three engines.
--
-- The spelling NO per-module scope decision can reach: `entry` imports only a
-- VALUE of the record.  A-2.6's `memberOverlayDecl` and unit 2's
-- `applyRecordScopeOverrides` both key on an import member that NAMES a
-- constructor or its type, and `mkA` is neither — so both declined to the
-- bare-name floor and module load order picked `zrmod`'s unrelated `Cfg`.
--
-- Before: `Type mismatch: Cfg vs Cfg — … one comes from module 'armod', the other
-- from module 'zrmod'` plus `Float vs Int`, exit 1.  Measured first-hand on a
-- binary cold-built from `main` @ e93d38d9 (unit 2 merged), and the issue records
-- the same on `main` @ 1443870c and on PR #1372's branch.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS FIXTURE'S CONTROL IS ITS OWN SIBLING, NOT A FILE HERE.  The
-- discriminator that proved this was not a #1283 re-export residual is that the
-- DECLARING-module spelling (`import armod.{mkA}`, exactly what this file writes,
-- no re-exporter anywhere) failed too.  `record_scope_reexport_ident/` is the
-- re-export shape and stays green independently; if only that one is green this
-- row is not covered.
--
-- Hand-derived correct answer, independent of any engine: `mkA : Cfg` is armod's
-- record, whose `fld` is an `Int` and is `7`; `zrf 0 = 0`.  So `7`.
--
-- What makes it work now: `mkA`'s TYPE carries `armod` (that is how the old
-- diagnostic knew to say `armod`), and `resolveFieldRecord`'s arm (a) selects the
-- `RecordInfo` by that identity instead of by the string `"Cfg"`.  Nothing in this
-- file's import spelling is consulted, which is the point — there is nothing here
-- to key a scope decision on.
import armod.{mkA}
import zrmod.{zrf}

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn (intToString (mkA.fld + zrf 0))
